{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"Extragonadal germ cell tumors form from developing sperm or egg cells that travel from the gonads to other parts of the body.","source_span":"Extragonadal germ cell tumors form from developing sperm or egg cells that travel from the gonads to other parts of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"\"Extragonadal\" means outside of the gonads (sex organs).","source_span":"\" Extragonadal\" means outside of the gonads (sex organs)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"Extragonadal germ cell tumors usually begin in organs such as the pineal gland in the brain, the mediastinum, or the retroperitoneum.","source_span":"usually begin in organs such as the pineal gland in the brain, in the mediastinum (area between the lungs), or in the retroperitoneum (the back wall of the abdomen)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"Benign extragonadal germ cell tumors are called benign teratomas and are more common than malignant extragonadal germ cell tumors.","source_span":"Benign extragonadal germ cell tumors are called benign teratomas. These are more common than malignant extragonadal germ cell tumors"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"Malignant extragonadal germ cell tumors are divided into two types: nonseminoma and seminoma.","source_span":"Malignant extragonadal germ cell tumors are divided into two types, nonseminoma and seminoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"Nonseminomas tend to grow and spread more quickly than seminomas.","source_span":"Nonseminomas tend to grow and spread more quickly than seminomas."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"If untreated, malignant extragonadal germ cell tumors may spread to the lungs, lymph nodes, bones, liver, or other parts of the body.","source_span":"If untreated, malignant extragonadal germ cell tumors may spread to the lungs, lymph nodes, bones, liver, or other parts of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"Risk factors for malignant extragonadal germ cell tumors include being male, being age 20 or older, and having Klinefelter syndrome.","source_span":"Risk factors for malignant extragonadal germ cell tumors include the following: - Being male. - Being age 20 or older. - Having Klinefelter syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of extragonadal germ cell tumors include chest pain, breathing problems, cough, fever, headache, change in bowel habits, feeling very tired, trouble walking, and trouble seeing or moving the eyes.","source_span":"Chest pain. - Breathing problems. - Cough. - Fever. - Headache. - Change in bowel habits. - Feeling very tired. - Trouble walking. - Trouble in seeing or moving the eyes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"Three tumor markers used to detect extragonadal germ cell tumors are Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), Beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (beta-hCG), and Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH).","source_span":"The following three tumor markers are used to detect extragonadal germ cell tumor: - Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP). - Beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (-hCG). - Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for extragonadal germ cell tumors depend on tumor type (nonseminoma/seminoma), size and location, AFP/hCG/LDH blood levels, whether it has spread, treatment response, and whether newly diagnosed or recurred.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options depend on the following: - Whether the tumor is nonseminoma or seminoma. - The size of the tumor and where it is in the body. - The blood levels of AFP, -hCG, and LDH. - Whether the tumor has spread to other parts of the body. - The way the tumor responds to initial treatment. - Whether the tumor has just been diagnosed or has recurred (come back)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What does the term \"extragonadal\" mean in relation to germ cell tumors?","answer":"It means outside of the gonads (sex organs) — the tumor forms from germ cells that traveled elsewhere in the body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the two types of malignant extragonadal germ cell tumors?","answer":"Nonseminoma and seminoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","fact_refs":[9]}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Which three tumor markers are used to help detect extragonadal germ cell tumors?","answer":"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (beta-hCG), and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What are the risk factors for developing a malignant extragonadal germ cell tumor?","answer":"Being male, being age 20 or older, and having Klinefelter syndrome."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"In what parts of the body do extragonadal germ cell tumors usually begin growing?","answer":"They usually begin in the pineal gland in the brain, the mediastinum (area between the lungs), or the retroperitoneum (back wall of the abdomen)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000016_1","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What factors affect the prognosis of an extragonadal germ cell tumor?","answer":"Whether it is nonseminoma or seminoma, its size and location, blood levels of AFP/beta-hCG/LDH, whether it has spread, its response to initial treatment, and whether it is newly diagnosed or has recurred."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","statement":"Intraocular melanoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the eye.","source_span":"Intraocular melanoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the eye."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","statement":"Intraocular melanoma forms in the middle layer of the eye wall, called the uvea or uveal tract, which has three main parts: the iris, ciliary body, and choroid.","source_span":"The middle layer, where intraocular melanoma forms, is called the uvea or uveal tract, and has three main parts: - Iris ... - Ciliary body ... - Choroid"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","statement":"Most intraocular melanomas begin in the choroid.","source_span":"Most intraocular melanomas begin in the choroid."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","statement":"Intraocular melanoma is the most common eye cancer in adults.","source_span":"It is the most common eye cancer in adults."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","statement":"Intraocular melanoma of the iris is usually a small tumor that grows slowly and rarely spreads to other parts of the body.","source_span":"Intraocular melanoma of the iris is usually a small tumor that grows slowly and rarely spreads to other parts of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","statement":"Risk factors for intraocular melanoma include fair complexion (fair skin that freckles/burns easily, blue/green/light-colored eyes), older age, and being white.","source_span":"Risk factors for intraocular melanoma include the following: - Having a fair complexion...- Older age. - Being white."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","statement":"Signs of intraocular melanoma include blurred vision, floaters or flashes of light, a dark spot on the iris, a change in pupil size/shape, and a change in eyeball position.","source_span":"Blurred vision or other change in vision. - Floaters ... or flashes of light. - A dark spot on the iris. - A change in the size or shape of the pupil. - A change in the position of the eyeball in the eye socket."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","statement":"A biopsy of the tumor is rarely needed to diagnose intraocular melanoma.","source_span":"A biopsy of the tumor is rarely needed to diagnose intraocular melanoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the three main parts of the uvea, the middle layer of the eye where intraocular melanoma forms?","answer":"The iris, the ciliary body, and the choroid."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Where do most intraocular melanomas begin?","answer":"Most begin in the choroid."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What risk factors are associated with intraocular melanoma?","answer":"A fair complexion (fair skin that burns/freckles easily, light-colored eyes), older age, and being white."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are common signs and symptoms of intraocular melanoma?","answer":"Blurred vision, floaters or flashes of light, a dark spot on the iris, a change in pupil size or shape, and a change in eyeball position."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Is a biopsy usually required to diagnose intraocular melanoma?","answer":"No, a biopsy is rarely needed to diagnose intraocular melanoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000017_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How does intraocular melanoma of the iris typically behave compared to melanoma in other parts of the uvea?","answer":"Iris melanoma is usually a small tumor that grows slowly and rarely spreads to other parts of the body, unlike ciliary body or choroid melanoma which are often larger and more likely to spread."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"Retinoblastoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the retina.","source_span":"Retinoblastoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the retina."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"Retinoblastoma occurs most often in children younger than 2 years.","source_span":"it occurs most often in children younger than 2 years"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"Retinoblastoma may be unilateral (one eye) or bilateral (both eyes) and rarely spreads from the eye to nearby tissue or other parts of the body.","source_span":"The cancer may be in one eye (unilateral) or in both eyes (bilateral). Retinoblastoma rarely spreads from the eye to nearby tissue or other parts of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"A child is thought to have heritable retinoblastoma if there is a family history, a mutation in the RB1 gene, more than one tumor or tumors in both eyes, or a tumor in one eye with the child younger than 1 year.","source_span":"A child is thought to have the heritable form of retinoblastoma when one of the following is true: - There is a family history of retinoblastoma. - There is a certain mutation (change) in the RB1 gene. ... - There is more than one tumor in the eye or there is a tumor in both eyes. - There is a tumor in one eye and the child is younger than 1 year."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"Most cases of retinoblastoma are the nonheritable form.","source_span":"Most cases of retinoblastoma are the nonheritable form."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"After heritable retinoblastoma is diagnosed and treated, regular eye exams to check for new tumors are usually done every 2 to 4 months for at least 28 months.","source_span":"Regular eye exams to check for new tumors are usually done every 2 to 4 months for at least 28 months."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"Brothers or sisters of a child with retinoblastoma should have regular eye exams by an ophthalmologist until age 3 to 5 years, unless known they lack the RB1 gene change.","source_span":"Brothers or sisters of a child with retinoblastoma should have regular eye exams by an ophthalmologist until age 3 to 5 years, unless it is known that the brother or sister does not have the RB1 gene change."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"Trilateral retinoblastoma is when retinoblastoma and a pineal brain tumor occur at the same time; the brain tumor is usually diagnosed between 20 and 36 months of age.","source_span":"When retinoblastoma and a brain tumor occur at the same time, it is called trilateral retinoblastoma. The brain tumor is usually diagnosed between 20 and 36 months of age."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"CT scans are usually not used for routine retinoblastoma screening in order to avoid exposing the child to ionizing radiation.","source_span":"CT (computerized tomography) scans are usually not used for routine screening in order to avoid exposing the child to ionizing radiation."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"Heritable retinoblastoma increases the child's risk of other cancers in later years, such as lung cancer, bladder cancer, or melanoma.","source_span":"Heritable retinoblastoma also increases the child's risk of other types of cancer such as lung cancer, bladder cancer, or melanoma in later years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","statement":"A common symptom of retinoblastoma is that the pupil of the eye appears white instead of red when light shines into it, which may be seen in flash photographs.","source_span":"Pupil of the eye appears white instead of red when light shines into it. This may be seen in flash photographs of the child."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"At what age does retinoblastoma occur most often?","answer":"It occurs most often in children younger than 2 years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What conditions indicate a child has the heritable form of retinoblastoma?","answer":"A family history of retinoblastoma, a mutation in the RB1 gene, more than one tumor or tumors in both eyes, or a tumor in one eye when the child is younger than 1 year."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is trilateral retinoblastoma and when is the associated brain tumor usually diagnosed?","answer":"Trilateral retinoblastoma is when retinoblastoma occurs together with a pineal brain tumor; the brain tumor is usually diagnosed between 20 and 36 months of age."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How often should eye exams be done after heritable retinoblastoma is diagnosed and treated, and for how long?","answer":"Regular eye exams to check for new tumors are usually done every 2 to 4 months for at least 28 months."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","fact_refs":[7]}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Why are CT scans usually avoided for routine screening of retinoblastoma?","answer":"To avoid exposing the child to ionizing radiation."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000018_1","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What is a classic visible sign of retinoblastoma that can show up in flash photographs?","answer":"The pupil of the eye appears white instead of red when light shines into it."}
